"""— FIELD GUIDE · Vol. I —
SURVIVING THE ISLAND

You wake on the Wildmere with a box, a dream, and a growling gut.
Eat. The red berries by the shore keep a body moving; raw food
spoils within the hour — cook it, dry it, or staff an ice box
with a cooling kin or watch your larder turn to mush. And what
rots is not lost: a farming kin at the compost bin turns scraps
and straw into fertilizer that makes a field surge for five
minutes. The island wastes nothing if you work it right. And
where the shore runs shallow, set a fish trap in the surf — a
watering kin will pull fish from it all day; fry the catch for
a meal that puts wind in your boots. A skep of bees rounds out
the pantry — honey bakes into buns that quicken the step. And
a well sunk near the beds keeps them watered in fair skies —
the same quarter rain gives, without waiting on the weather —
and the only drink a drought respects.
And the sea feeds those who ask: cast a line from any shore —
fish bite harder when it rains, and once in a long while the
water gives back a corked bottle holding somebody's map.

The island keeps a clock. Daylight is for building; night is
when the dark element kin wake and hunt wide. Cold bites past
dusk — stand near a fire, hug a fire kin, or wear hide. And if
the sky won't cooperate, a weather shrine takes lumenite in
exchange for a word with the heavens: sun, rain, thunder,
drought, and back around — choose the sky your yard needs.
Where does lumenite come from? The drift cargo you washed
up with covers the first kinbox; after that, swing at the
rocks — the odd shard glints in the rubble of stone, coal,
and gold ore alike. Mind the parch: fields beyond a well's
shadow work a fifth slower under a dry sky, while fire kin
come into their own.

The far north freezes around the clock. The far south scorches
by day and snaps cold at night. Pack warm fur and a tropical
weave before you go looking for the legendary dens — or pack the
kettle instead: a spicy stew keeps an inner hearth burning through
any chill, and a cold mint salad lets the noon sun slide off. For
the dark miles between camps, a miner's cap burns a carbide lamp
on your hat — both hands free for the pick. No cap? A bowl of
glowcap soup does for a few hours: cave mushrooms simmered
down, and the light comes from inside you.

Fall in the field and your cargo stays where you fell — the
island marks the spot with a stone, and the pack waits for
you to come back for it. Wilds will not touch it, time will
not rot it, and even a save-and-leave keeps it pinned. What
drops can be lost is the ordinary scatter from broken rocks
and felled beasts — that decays. Your death-pack does not.""",
"""— VOL. II —
THE ART OF THE BALL

Weaken first, capture second. A kin at low health and any
ailment catches far easier; a strike from behind tilts the
odds further. Watch the ball — three shakes, then the verdict.

A kin's LEVEL is the real gate. A lv5 fenjelly folds to a
plain orb; a lv30 alpha shrugs it off. The orb ladder
exists for a reason: kin < mega < giga < ultra < legendary.
Squeamish about over-swinging? The Ring of Mercy keeps your
blows honest — a wild kin under that charm can be whittled
to its last breath, never past it. And the tenth of any
species is a rite: once the kindex counts ten of a kind,
every one you keep fights a tenth sharper forever.
Orb Works and Giga Works lines feed the habit — the second
line stretches electric organs three times further.

Orbs must hit home — nothing is spent on a miss. The
launcher flings the selected ball downrange. Sleeping kin
catch asleep; they wake swinging. Shy prey? Toss a bait mash
ahead of you: hunger beats caution, and the first snout to
the pile holds still while it chews. Pair the bait with a
leaf-litter snare and the meal walks to you — whatever steps
in is held fast for a few heartbeats, one bite and the spring
is spent. Or take the quiet road entirely: a tranquilizer gun
puffs a venom-coated dart that drops a wild kin mid-stride —
sleeping prey catches like it dreams of being caught.

And keep your hand steady: each capture that sticks feeds a
quiet streak in your throwing arm — every success in a row
pays a little more experience, up to half again. One kin
breaking free snaps the run. A calm trapper is a rich one.""",
"""— VOL. III —
PARTY, BOX AND BENCH

Five slots on the belt, the rest in the box. Keys 1-5 send a
kin out; press again to recall. Only one ball on the field —
that is the law of the islands. Seal the Wildmere Accord and
the belt grows a sixth slot (key 6).

The deployed kin takes orders: v cycles AUTO / AGGRESSIVE /
PASSIVE. It favors your mark — hit something and it presses
the same target for a few breaths.

A benched shadelump still fires at your fight. Nibjelly and
piplump squeeze milk on command, fenlump lays — the ranch
walks in your pocket. Fenjelly rolls under you as an absorb
bubble; corlmite, with its launcher, IS the ammunition.

Fellow keepers on the same island carry their own belt and
box — the yard is shared, the kin are not. A catch answers
to the hand that threw the ball: another keeper's kin takes
no orders from you, shares no bench with yours, and drops its
haul in its own master's bag. Sail home mid-visit and your
field kin balls up under your name, waiting for your return.""",
"""— VOL. IV —
THE BASE AND ITS WORKERS

Everything on the yard runs on kin with the right WORK
SUITABILITY: kindling lights furnaces, watering grows wheat,
handiwork feeds the assembly lines, transporting hauls the
harvest to chests. Idle base kin find empty posts on their
own; f assigns one by hand.

Kin are not machines — but they do love a scratch. A petted
kin works a tenth lighter for the next minute (and the pat
itself tops up SAN). Hunger drains them — keep the feed box
stocked. SAN frays under grind — hot springs, beds, and the
monitoring stand's gentler shifts keep minds whole. Stand in
the steam yourself and the water knits your own cuts too. Sick kin
need medicine; the box heals the KO'd on a timer. And mind the
depressed ones at the bottom of the tank — left at empty SAN
with no medicine in reach, they snap and chew the very walls
they built. It passes, but the bill is yours.

Sleep heals: a real bed knits wounds thrice as fast as the
floor. Dark kin keep night hours and nap by day. And a kin
with no trade should not idle — set a training dummy in the
yard and watch it spar itself stronger between raids. And when
the horns sound, a posted guard earns its keep — kin on a
guard platform patrol a wider beat than the yard crew. Veterans
serve twice: post one to the drill hall and, each time the bell
tolls, it will pass one of its own moves to a youngster standing
nearby — up to three lessons per pupil. And a roped pit settles
the rest: pair two kin in the sparring ring and every bell ends
in a bout — the winner banks the most, both spend a little spirit.
And a war banner does for the yard what a drum does for a march —
kin working within sight of its colors put on a little more pace.""",
"""— VOL. V —
BREEDING, EGGS AND LUCK

A ranch pair — one male, one female — produces an egg of a
species written in the old pairing tables. The incubator
hatches it; a nearby campfire hurries the shell along.

Parents pass down passive traits and even learned skills —
breed for the kin you want. Rare HUGE EGGS (nests, alphas,
the odd grotto chest, the shadier merchants) always hatch a
LUCKY variant: bigger, stronger, twice the drops, and coins
to boot. You'll hear the chime when one spawns. Short on
eggs? The brass kin gacha takes thirty gold a pull and
rattles one out — mostly plain, sometimes huge.

Condense spare kin of the same species for star ranks;
the statue feeds their soul.""",
"""— VOL. VI —
STEEL, STITCHES AND THE ELEMENTS

Elements interplay: water soaks, and a soaked target takes
heavier lightning. Fire burns and melts a freeze early —
but a frozen foe that takes a HIT shatters for bonus damage.
Same-element strikes bite deeper (STAB); rain feeds water
moves, a thunderstorm feeds spark, a drought feeds flame.

Gear wears. Weapons and armor both grind down on use until
they snap — mend them at their station for half the recipe.
Bosses and alphas drop SCHEMATICS; keep the paper in your
bag and the bench rebuilds a meaner Mk. II.

Eating a proper meal buffs attack, defense or work for a
few minutes. Buffs stack with kin auras.""",
"""— VOL. VII —
PEOPLE OF THE ISLANDS

Wandering merchants come and go — fair prices for supplies,
black-market rates for kin eggs, skill fruit, and the odd
HUGE egg. Attack one at your peril: the Iron Pact pays
bounty hunters, and wanted squads find you. Some marks carry
posted bounties — kill them for coin.

The Iron Pact raids too. Towers, walls and spikes hold the
line; a horn warns you before the wave. Clear the raid boss
and the spoils are real: legendary orbs, ancient parts,
schematics. Raise a lightning rod and pray for a loud sky —
each strike banks an electric organ for the orb lines.
Feeling brave? Alphas sometimes carry a Boss
Sigil — the merchant sells them under the counter, and the
deep grottos hoard them. Use one at your own kinbox to call
the raid boss down yourself. Out in the deep wilds the
Iron Pact keeps poacher camps: guards around an iron cage,
and some unlucky local behind the bars. Break the lock and
the captive scurries into your kinbox — a free catch, if you
can take the escort.

Chests hide in grottos and lairs; the third chamber guards
something old. But mind the boxes that sit too still — some
are only chest-shaped. Open one and it opens back, grinning,
and the deeper the room the hungrier the joke. Out past the
second ridge the ground keeps older things: bone-heaps of
kin that died before names were given. Dig a fossil bed and
the deep past pays out in ancient parts — one dig, then the
ground is quiet again.

And keep a notice board by the gate: each dawn a fresh
request goes up — fill it before dark for coin and credit
worth more than the goods. Some mornings the guild posts a
cull instead: thin a named species and the board pays out in
the field — a bag or a box both count as thinning.""",
"""— VOL. VIII —
MAPS, WILDS AND THE LONG GAME

Your map fills as you walk — fog lifts only where you've
been. Waypoints blink you between bases; more kinboxes mean
more yards, all sharing the one box. A trader may sell you
a faded treasure chart — follow the gold X on your map and
dig where it lands. Too tired to walk? Raise a spyglass
tower — every half minute the brass glass sweeps a wider
arc and charts the wilds from your own gate.

The expedition post sends a gatherer off-map for ten minutes
and brings back a haul keyed to its element. The statues
upgrade your perks; the memory-wipe draught refunds them.
Day-counting raids grow meaner; nights level the wilds.

The island's herds move too. Every so often a pack from a
deeper band crosses the map — watch for the MIGRATION call
and you'll catch species you haven't earned the right to
walk to yet. The pack settles where it lands; get there
before the locals do.

Fill the kindex — each entry shows habitat, drops, and
work suitability. The islands remember everything you
catalog. Good hunting.""",
"""— VOL. IX —
THE PACT, THE SIGILS, THE TRIAL

The Iron Pact raised five spires across the Wildmere — rock
rings you can see from off-shore, each held by an alpha
guardian. Chart their ground and the spires ping violet on
your map. Break a guardian and the spire falls; every ruin
yields a Gauntlet Sigil, the tower's own dare.

Sound a sigil at your kinbox and the tower answers: three
waves, harder than anything the wilds field, closing with a
true boss. Clear it and the tower pays — orbs, effigies,
gold, and the next sigil. Lose the field and the question
ends unanswered; sound another sigil to try again.

Break all five spires and the island crowns you keeper.
A crowned keeper who sounds a sigil faces a fourth, apex
wave — the tower's last question — and the Wildmere's
final contract, the Accord, waits for the keeper who proves
every seal: the crown, the apex trial, and every legend's
heir caught from the deep dens. The Accord signs in gold
and opens a sixth place in your party.

And after the signature? The island keeps score: dens
re-call their heirs in a day and a half — favoring the
names you have yet to catch, a crowned keeper may keep
every legend for the roster. Apex trials pay out
whenever you can sound a sigil, the catalog counts to its
last page, stars condense, statues still teach. And the
merchant honors the contract: a sealed keeper's briefcase
always holds effigies — gold into souls, as often as you
can pay. The Wildmere is yours; spend it.""",
